DIRECTIVES
(UE) 2018/1673 OF THE EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT AND THE COUNCIL
of 23 October 2018
on combating money laundering by criminal law
The European Parliament and the Council of the European Union,
pursuant to the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union, and in particular its Article 83, paragraph 1,
having regard to the proposal of the European Commission,
after transmission of the legislative act to the national parliaments,
acting in accordance with the ordinary legislative procedure (1),
considering that:
(1) Money laundering and the financing linked to this phenomenon, as well as terrorism financing and organized crime, remain significant problems at Union level, causing damage to the integrity, stability and reputation of the financial sector and threatening the internal market and the internal security of the Union. In order to address these problems and to supplement and strengthen the application of Directive (UE) 2015/849 of the European Parliament and the Council (2), the present directive aims to combat money laundering through criminal law, enabling more effective and rapid cross-border cooperation between competent authorities.
(2) Measures adopted at the national level or even at the Union level, without taking into account international coordination and cooperation, would have very (5) The definition of criminal activities constituting key offenses in the field of money laundering should be uniform in all Member States. Member States should ensure that all offenses punishable by imprisonment under the present directive are considered as key offenses in money laundering. In addition, Member States should include a range of offenses relevant to each of the categories of offenses listed in the present directive, unless the application of these penalty thresholds already provides for this. In such cases, Member States should be able to determine the scope of offenses within each category. When a category of offenses, such as terrorism or an environmental offense, includes offenses defined in acts of the Union, the present directive should refer to these legal acts. However, Member States should consider any offense defined in these legal acts as constituting a money laundering offense. Any participation in the commission of a money laundering offense, punishable by sanctions and elevated to the level of a criminal offense under national law, should also be considered an criminal activity under this directive. In cases where Union law allows Member States to provide for sanctions other than penal sanctions, these should be considered equivalent to money laundering offenses for the purposes of this directive. (12) For effective penal measures against money laundering, a condemnation should be possible without the need to determine precisely the criminal activity that generated the assets, or for there to be a prior or concurrent condemnation of this criminal activity, while taking into account all circumstances and relevant evidence. Member States should be able to ensure this through means other than legislation, in accordance with their national legal systems. Proceedings for money laundering should not be impeded by the fact that the criminal activity was committed in another Member State or in a third country, provided the conditions set out in this directive are met.

(13) This directive aims to make money laundering an criminal offence when it is committed intentionally and knowing that the assets derived from a criminal activity. In this context, this directive should not make a distinction between situations where the assets originate directly from a criminal activity and situations where they originate indirectly from such an activity, in accordance with the broad definition of "proceeds" provided by Directive 2014/42/EU of the European Parliament and the Council (1). In each case, when assessing whether the assets are derived from a criminal activity and whether the person had knowledge of this, all circumstances should be taken into account. (17) Given the mobility of offenders and criminal proceeds, as well as the complexity of transborder investigations required to combat money laundering, all Member States should establish their competence to allow competent authorities to investigate such activities and bring charges against them. In this context, Member States should ensure that their competence covers situations where an offence is committed using information and communication technologies from their territory, whether or not the technologies are located there.

(18) Pursuant to the Council Framework Decision 2009/948/JAI (1) and the Council Decision 2002/187/JAI (2), competent authorities of two or more Member States conducting parallel criminal proceedings for the same facts involving the same person must, with Eurojust's assistance, engage in direct consultations, to ensure in particular that all offences relevant to this Directive are subject to prosecution.

