Article 2
Definitions

For the purposes of this directive, the following terms mean:
1) "criminal activity": any form of criminal participation in the commission of any offense that, according to national law, is punishable by a term of imprisonment or a measure of restraint of a maximum duration exceeding one year, or, in Member States whose legal system sets a minimum threshold for offenses, any offense punishable by a term of imprisonment or a measure of restraint of a minimum duration exceeding six months. In any case, the following offenses are considered criminal activities:
a) membership in a criminal organization and extortion rackets, including any offense provided for in the EU Council Framework Decision 2008/841/JHA;
b) terrorism, including any offense provided for in Directive (EU) 2017/541 of the European Parliament and of the Council (1);
c) human trafficking and smuggling of migrants, including any offense provided for in Directive 2011/36/EU of the European Parliament and a) money counterfeiting, including any offence provided for in European Parliament and Council Directive 2014/62/UE (1);
b) counterfeiting and piracy of products;
c) environmental offences, including any offence provided for in European Parliament and Council Directive 2008/99/CE (2) or in European Parliament and Council Directive 2009/123/CE (3);
d) murder and grievous bodily harm;
e) kidnapping, sequestration, and hostage-taking;
f) theft with or without violence;
g) smuggling;
h) fiscal offences related to direct and indirect taxes, as defined by national law;
i) extortion;
j) forgery;
k) piracy;
l) insider trading and market manipulation, including any offence provided for in European Parliament and Council Directive 2014/57/EU (4);
m) cybercrime, including any offence provided for in European Parliament and Council Directive 2013/40/EU (5);

2. "goods": all assets of any nature, tangible or intangible, movable or immovable, as well as documents or legal instruments, in any form, including electronic or digital form, Members States take necessary measures to ensure:
a) that a condemnation for offenses under paragraphs 1 and 2 is possible even in the absence of a prior or concurrent condemnation for the criminal activity from which the proceeds originate;
b) that a condemnation for offenses under paragraphs 1 and 2 is possible when it is established that the proceeds came from a criminal activity, without the need to establish all the factual elements or circumstances related to this criminal activity, including the identity of the author;
c) that offenses under paragraphs 1 and 2 cover proceeds from conduct that took place on the territory of another Member State or of a third country, when such conduct would constitute a criminal activity if it had taken place on the national territory.

4. Regarding paragraph 3, point c, of the present article, Members States may also request that the conduct in question constitutes a criminal offense under the national law of the other Member State or of the third country where the conduct took place, except when such conduct constitutes one of the offenses listed in Article 2, point 1, letters a) to e) and h), and as defined in the applicable Union law.

5. Members States take necessary measures to ensure that the conduct referred to in paragraph 1, points a) and b), constitute criminal offenses punishable by sanctions when committed a) an authority to make decisions on behalf of a legal entity; or
b) an authority to exercise control within a legal entity.
2. Member States take the necessary measures to enable a legal entity to be held liable when the failure to supervise or control, on the part of a person referred to in paragraph 1 of this article, has allowed any offence under article 3, paragraphs 1 and 5, and article 4 to be committed, on behalf of said legal entity, by a person subject to its authority.
3. The liability of legal entities under paragraphs 1 and 2 of this article does not exclude the pursuit of penal proceedings against the natural persons who are authors, instigators, or accomplices of any offence under article 3, paragraphs 1 and 5, and article 4.

Article 8

Sanctions against legal entities

Member States take the necessary measures to ensure that any legal entity held liable under article 7 is subject to effective, proportionate, and deterrent sanctions, which may include penalties and non-penal sanctions, such as:

a) exclusion from public benefits or aid;
b) exclusion from temporary or permanent access to public funding, including calls for tenders, subsidies, and concessions;
